// How each species branches, by number, its knot style: [branches per whorl
// (up to 6), small branches between whorls (up to 2), rise of a dominant
// branch at the pith in mm per mm, scatter]; see whorlTime. Measured: Scots
// pine's 4 to 6 branches and rise (Bjorklund and Moberg 1999, Pietila 1989),
// eastern white pine's 6 to 7 (USFS), Douglas fir's 6 to 7 and 0.27 to 0.41
// (Osborne and Maguire 2015), Sitka spruce's 4 to 5 whorl and 1 to 5 between,
// rising 0.31 and 0.08 (Achim 2006), sugi's angles (0.33 to 0.42, Kano 1966).
// Hinoki and the cedars branch diffusely, without whorls (Edelin 1981, Parker
// and Johnson 1987), so their branches scatter; sugi and yew in loose
// pseudo-whorls, yew's nearly flat. Hardwoods' knots are sparse and scattered,
// two to a spacing. The counts past what is measured are judged.
