// How each species takes a stain, by number: [earlywood, latewood, pores,
// blotch]. The first three scale a stain's uptake where the wood is soft,
// hard, and in the open pores (for a pigment); blotch is how much more it
// takes in the patches where it blotches, as cherry, maple, birch and pine do.
// From anatomy: softwoods' soft earlywood takes 1.3 and the hard latewood 0.5,
// so a stain turns their rings the other way round; ring-porous woods' pores
// hold three times as much pigment; the oily and very dense woods take less
// throughout. Sugar maple takes 1, as the wood a stain's colour is given on.
// Judged.
stainUptake = [
  [1.1, 0.9, 3, 0.1], // whiteOak
  [1.1, 0.9, 3, 0.1], // redOak
  [0.8, 0.8, 1.6, 0.1], // liveOak
